<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylonian captivity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/597-bce">597</a>–<a href="/years/539-bce">539 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Babylonian captivity was the period in the sixth century BCE when many inhabitants of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, following the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The exile ended after the Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, when Cyrus allowed the exiles to return. It had a profound influence on the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity.jpg" alt="Babylonian captivity" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Tissot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tissot_The_Flight_of_the_Prisoners.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The destruction of Jerusalem in 587/586 BCE and the <b>Babylonian captivity</b> profoundly shaped the development of Judaism, accelerating the shift from a temple-centred, territorially rooted religion to a portable, text-centred faith capable of surviving in exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars think the Mesopotamian myth spread west to the Hebrews, who may also have met it during the <b>Babylonian captivity</b>; others suppose a common ancestor, and Conrad Hyers read Genesis as written against such myths.</blockquote><a href="/songs/enuma-elis">Enūma Eliš</a></li><li><blockquote>Its four middle stanzas retell four deliverances of the Jewish people: from Pharaoh in the Exodus, from Nebuchadnezzar at the end of the <b>Babylonian captivity</b>, from Haman in Persia (the miracle of Purim) and from Antiochus, in the revolt against Greek rule commemorated by Hanukkah.</blockquote><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the poem as a prophecy: of Samuel's later role as a prophet, of a great-grandson of Hannah who would be a musician in the Jerusalem Temple, of Sennacherib's destruction of the Kingdom of Israel, of the fall of Nebuchadnezzar and of the end of the <b>Babylonian Captivity</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-hannah">Song of Hannah</a></li><li><blockquote>It recalls the <b>Babylonian captivity</b> after the destruction of Solomon's Temple in Jerusalem in 586 BC: the exiled Hebrews sing of their homeland.</blockquote><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</a></li></ul></section>
